<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sexenio Democrático</h1><p class="st-meta">1868 CE – 1874 CE · capital Madrid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Six turbulent years of revolution in Spain, from the fall of Isabella II through an elected king and a republic to the return of the Bourbons.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In September 1868 a military rising led by Generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-prim">Juan Prim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-serrano-1st-duke-of-la-torre">Francisco Serrano</a> and Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-topete">Juan Bautista Topete</a>, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution-spain">Glorious Revolution</a>, drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-ii-of-spain">Isabella II</a> into exile. A provisional government called elections by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a>, and the Constitution of 1869 declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With the throne vacant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-serrano-1st-duke-of-la-torre">Serrano</a> acted as regent while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-prim">Prim</a> searched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for a king, a search that helped trigger the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes">Cortes</a> chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeo-i-of-spain">Amadeo of Savoy</a>, but Prim was assassinated in December 1870, days before he arrived. Amadeo faced Carlist rebellion, war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and fractious parties, and abdicated in February 1873.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-first-republic">Spanish First Republic</a> followed, collapsing into the Pavía coup of January 1874. In December 1874 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsenio-martinez-campos">Martínez Campos</a> proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xii">Alfonso XII</a> at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saguntum">Sagunto</a>, opening <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/restoration-spain">Restoration Spain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sexenio-democratico.jpg" alt="Sexenio Democrático" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: Tomás Padró Pedret · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caricatura_etapas_del_sexenio_La_Flaca_1874.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1868 CE – 1874 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madrid</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francisco Serrano, Juan Prim, Amadeo I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Democratic Sexennium, Kingdom of Spain under Amadeo I</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1871&amp;focus=sexenio-democratico" aria-label="Sexenio Democrático on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4589 733 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sexenio Democrático lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4909 989L4909 989M4909 989L4909 989M4909 989L4910 989M4909 989L4960 945"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4909 989h0M4909 989h0M4910 989h0M4960 945h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Madrid. <a href="/atlas?year=1871&amp;focus=sexenio-democratico">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
